    "abstract": "Climate-driven intensification of forest wildfires releases reactive gases that reduce the atmosphere\u2019s oxidation capacity, thereby increasing methane concentrations and amplifying warming. Here our integrated fire\u2013atmospheric chemistry modelling shows that the resulting additional warming between the 2000s and the 2050s rivals that from wetland methane emissions and fire-related carbon dioxide under an intermediate climate pathway. This finding highlights the critical role of atmospheric chemistry in regulating fire\u2013climate feedbacks. In a warming climate, intensified wildfire emissions reduce global atmospheric oxidation capacity and amplify methane accumulation, leading to a positive climate feedback, according to an analysis of model projections from the 2000s to the 2050s.",
    "field": "Climate-chemistry interactions",
    "problem": "Existing climate change assessments inadequately quantify a critical positive feedback where reactive gases from wildfires reduce atmospheric oxidation capacity, thereby increasing methane concentrations and amplifying warming, because this fire-chemistry interaction is insufficiently explored in models.",
    "method": "This study uses an integrated fire (CESM-RESFire) and atmospheric chemistry (GEOS-Chem) modelling framework to quantify how climate-driven increases in forest fire emissions reduce atmospheric oxidation, thereby amplifying methane concentrations and climate warming.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify the additional climate warming from increased methane concentrations, which result from intensified forest fires reducing the atmosphere's oxidation capacity, using integrated fire-atmospheric chemistry modelling between the 2000s and the 2050s.",
